    "text": "I did not propose a contract change. The file is missing `coverage_start`, and nothing in the evidence shows that was deliberate.\n\n- **What changed:** `elig_20261021.csv` has 24 rows and 7 of the 8 declared columns. `coverage_start` (a required date with min 1990) is missing. The other columns are unchanged in name, order and type, and every rule passes on them. No columns were added. The quarantine sample shows the header `member_id,first_name,last_name,dob,sex,plan_code,coverage_end,patient_ref`.\n- **Why I did not propose:** The only way to accept this file is to drop `coverage_start` or make it optional. That loosens a required date, and the file gives no reason to think the old rule is wrong for good data. There is no notice from the vendor, no prior file to compare against, and the feed history has only this one file. A column removed from an export template without anyone deciding to remove it looks like an accidental drop. That is the triage case (`request_resend`), not a contract change.\n- **What would change my view:** Confirmation from vendor-ops (owner `vendor-ops@example.com`) that the vendor intentionally stopped sending `coverage_start`. If they confirm it, I can draft a drift contract. It would need to explain the loosening, and I would still want the downstream consumers of that date checked first. If the vendor confirms it was a mistake, the fix is a resend of the file with the column.\n\nThe contract is unchanged (version 1), and the file's quarantine stays open. Dry-run counts: 0 accepted, 0 rejected, since the file was quarantined at file level before row checks.",
